  "worked_solution": "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n${A}^{T}=A$\nbut \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n${B}^{T}=B$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ \\frac{2}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{T}\\ne C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only.\"",
